          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#model_structure_on_simplicial_props"} Model Structure on Simplicial PROPs
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#bialgebras"} Bialgebras
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#endomorphism_prop"} Endomorphism PROP
              %li
                %a{:href => "#relation_to_properads_and_polycategories"} Relation to properads and polycategories
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em PROP
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            generated by a single
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %em PROducts and Permutations
          %p
            PROPs are used to describe a given sort of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+structure"} algebraic structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            suitable for non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            contexts. In this respect they are similar to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operads"} operads
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong PROP
              is a strict
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              where every object is of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^{\otimes n} = x \otimes x \otimes \cdots \otimes x
            %p
              for a single object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 0
          %p
            There is also a notion of colored PROP akin to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colored+operads"} colored operads
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/toddtrimble/published/multisorted+Lawvere+theories"} multisorted Lawvere theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            (see Remark 2.2.14 of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Yau"} Yau
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong colored PROP
              with set of colors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℭ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{C}
              is a strict symmetric monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,\odot)
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
              of objects is freely generated by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℭ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{C}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong morphism of PROPs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Q
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:Q\to T
              is just a strict symmetric monoidal functor that takes the generators for the objects of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              to the generators of the objects of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              to the colors of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi c
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Col
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Col
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_c\colon Col(Q)\to Col(T)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{x}=\{x_1,\ldots,x_n\}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{y}=\{y_1,\ldots,y_m\}
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Col
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Col(Q)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi m
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo ,
                      %mover
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_m\colon Q(\vec{x},\vec{y})\to T(\phi_c(\vec{x}),\phi_c(\vec{y}))
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_c
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_m
              are of course required to preserve compositions, units and symmetries.
          %p
            Thus there is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong category of PROPs
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HR1"} HR1
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              Given a PROP
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              and a symmetric monoidal category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+functor"} symmetric monoidal functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : T \to C
            %p
              is called an
              %strong algebra
              or
              %strong model
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Alg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg(T,C)
            %ul
              %li
                symmetric monoidal functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : T \to C
                as objects,
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+natural+transformation"} symmetric monoidal natural transformations
                as morphisms.
          %p
            Note that all of the above definitions can be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            which yields the notion of enriched PROPs. For instance, we can have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicial
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topologically+enriched+category"} topological
            PROPs where the sets of morphisms are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %h2#model_structure_on_simplicial_props Model Structure on Simplicial PROPs
          %p
            First, in the case that we are working only with simplicial PROPs with a fixed set of colors (or, in other words, PROPs whose free monoids of objects all have the same generators), we have the following theorem of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#HR2"} HR2
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              There is a cofibrantly generated model structure on the category of simplicial PROPs with a fixed set of colors in which a morphism of simplicial PROPs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Q
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:Q\to T
              is a weak equivalence (resp. fibration) if for each simplicial set of morphisms the induced map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(\vec{x},\vec{y})\to T(\vec{x},\vec{y})
              is also a weak equivalence (resp. fibration).
          %p
            Recall now that simplicial PROPs admit an obvious forgetful functor to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            that factors through
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+categories"} simplicial categories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi sPROP
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0\colon sPROP\to Cat
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#HR2"} HR2
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              There is a cofibrantly generated model structure on the category of all simplicial PROPs where a morphism of PROPs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Q
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon Q\to T
              is a weak equivalence (resp. fibration) if:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the induced morphism of mapping complexes
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi m
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi Q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo ,
                        %mover
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_m\colon Q(\vec{x},\vec{y})\to T(\phi_c(\vec{x}),\phi_c(\vec{y}))
                  is a weak equivalence (resp.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
              %li
                %p
                  the functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                        %mi Q
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0\colon\pi_0Q\to \pi_0T
                  is a weak equivalence (resp.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
          %p
            Note that the model structure on simplicial PROPs is
            %em not
            the model structure on gets by lifting the model structure of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-operad"} simplicial operads
            along the free forgetful adjunction between simplicial operads and simplicial PROPs.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#bialgebras Bialgebras
          %p
            A perhaps paradigmatic example is that there is a
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            (or cooperad) whose algebras are bialgebras, so this is a genuine example that illustrates a gain in generality of props over operads.
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "#Pirashvili"} Pirashvili
            for some more details on this prop.
          %h3#endomorphism_prop Endomorphism PROP
          %p
            Given a set of colors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℭ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{C}
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            with a chosen collection of objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi c
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℭ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{X}=\{X_c\}_{c\in\mathfrak{C}}
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
